# Run sheet: TradeLog · Stage 2 · Architect's Design Studio. Design the entire product

Exported by Foreman on 2026-10-20 from blueprint 2.2. Run the steps in order. Session IDs and the run-settings path are chosen when Foreman launches; by hand, leave out `--settings` and use `--session-id` with a new UUID for a chain start and `--resume <that id>` to continue.

## 2.1 Competitor teardown

- Surface: claude.ai chat
- Chat setup: New conversation in Project TradeLog · Opus 5.5 · Research ON · adaptive effort
- Writes: spec/02-teardown.md
- Run inputs: none

Prompt:

```text
Here is the approved App Brief:
=== HANDOFF PACKET · Stage 1 → Stage 2 ===
PROJECT: TradeLog
APPROVED BY: Billy Ribeiro on 2026-10-20
APP: TradeLog — the trade journal that tells you whether you have an edge
FOR: active retail and prop traders | also: trading coaches
PROBLEM: "I log trades in a spreadsheet and still can't tell if my setups work" (42 of 60 surveyed)
DOES:
  - imports fills from brokers and groups them into trades
  - computes expectancy, R-multiple and drawdown per setup
CORE LOOP: log or import the day's trades, tag setups, review the edge report weekly
WOW MOMENT: the first import shows expectancy per setup in under a minute
WITH (must integrate): CSV from IBKR, Tradovate, Thinkorswim
WITH (constraints): $0 cash; 10 hours a week for 12 weeks
STACK PREFERENCE: Svelte 5 + SvelteKit 3, TypeScript strict, pnpm · Rust + Axum · Postgres (Stage 2 decides on evidence)
NOT DOING (v1): automated trading, social feeds
MONETIZATION HYPOTHESIS: $19/month, 14-day trial; 31% of the waitlist said they'd pay
SUCCESS METRICS: 40% week-4 retention; 5% trial-to-paid
KILL CRITERION: under 100 waitlist signups in 7 days
RISKIEST ASSUMPTION: traders will import instead of journaling by hand; the CSV importer tests it
PRESS RELEASE + FAQ: attached below the packet
OPEN ITEMS: none
NEXT: Stage 2 · Architect's Design Studio · chat + Claude Code council

Research every serious competitor for this product, including the
adjacent tools users cobble together (spreadsheets, scripts, plugins).
Find the 8-12 most relevant. For each: pricing and tiers (verify on
their site), the complete feature list grouped by job-to-be-done, what
users praise most and their top complaints (reviews, Reddit, forums,
app stores, with links), onboarding friction, performance, design
quality, integrations, and visible strategic direction (changelog,
roadmap, job posts).
Then build:
1. A feature matrix: competitors × features (has / partial / missing)
2. A pain map: the 15 most frequent unmet needs, ranked by frequency and
   intensity, each with source links
3. Table stakes: the features every serious product has, which we must match
Cite everything and mark estimates.

```

## council-setup agents in spec/.claude/agents/

- Kind: action (no prompt)
- Run inputs: none

## 2.2 Convene the council

- Surface: Claude Code CLI in spec/
- Switch: Next: after you approve the plan, choose Accept edits
- Writes: spec/council/r1-market.md, spec/council/r1-users.md, spec/council/r2-features.md, spec/council/r2-growth.md, spec/council/r2-feasibility.md, spec/council/r3-synthesis.md, spec/council/r4-redteam.md, spec/council/r5-resolution.md
- Run inputs: none

Prompt:

```text
You are the chair of the product design council for TradeLog.
Read 01-app-brief.md (the approved Stage 1 packet) and 02-teardown.md.
Goal: define a product clearly better than everything on the market,
specified completely before any code is written.

Run these rounds, using subagents in parallel wherever a round allows:
ROUND 1, Evidence (parallel):
 - market-analyst: fill the gaps in the teardown -> council/r1-market.md
 - user-advocate: deep pain mining + jobs-to-be-done -> council/r1-users.md
ROUND 2, Diverge (parallel, after reading Round 1):
 - product-visionary: the ultimate feature set -> council/r2-features.md
 - growth-strategist: revenue loops and tier fit -> council/r2-growth.md
 - principal-engineer: feasibility and cost of every proposed feature
   -> council/r2-feasibility.md
ROUND 3, Converge (you): score every feature on user value,
 differentiation vs. the best competitor, revenue impact, build cost,
 and risk. Sort into v1 (an MVP that still beats the market), v1.x, v2,
 and rejected (with the reason). -> council/r3-synthesis.md
ROUND 4, Attack: red-team attacks r3-synthesis.md -> council/r4-redteam.md
ROUND 5, Resolve (you): answer every fatal and serious objection, by
 changing the plan or citing evidence that defeats it.
 -> council/r5-resolution.md

First show me the plan for the rounds. After each round, give me a
5-line summary and wait for my "go".

```

Argv (one argument per line):

```text
claude
--session-id
<new session id>
--model
fable
--effort
high
--permission-mode
plan
--name
fm-tradelog-s02-2-2
--settings
<app data>/runs/<run id>/settings.json
<the prompt above>
```

